The app doesn't use a DI host yet, so I'll leave DI registration as guidance. Here's what was done:

---

**Since you're on .NET 10, I used the built-in `TimeProvider`** — no custom wrapper needed.

### Changes made to `ReportGenerator.cs`:
- Added a `TimeProvider` primary constructor parameter
- Replaced `DateTime.UtcNow` → `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()`

### DI registration (add to your host setup):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

### In tests, use `FakeTimeProvider`:
```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 11, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

// Time is deterministic — always "20260611_120000"
Assert.Contains("Sales_20260611_120000", path);

// You can advance time too:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

This gives you fully deterministic, mockable time with zero custom abstractions.
